-- POSITIVE DISCRIMINATOR 2 of 2 for nary_sig_requires_decoy: the decoy is KEPT and the
-- winning instance's `requires` context is REMOVED.  The complementary one-dimension
-- change to its sibling, so the two together separate the decoy from the `requires`.
--
-- HAND-DERIVED EXPECTED VALUE -- 107, from DICT-SEMANTICS §3, not captured.
--   Goal π = Ix Int (Box Int); IE = { Ix Int Bool, Ix Int (Box c) }.  `Ix Int Bool`
--   differs from π at argument 1 and is not in the matching set; `Ix Int (Box c)`
--   matches under φ = [Int/c].  Singleton, so `inst` applies with an EMPTY context.
--   Body: 107.
--
-- WHAT IT LOCALISES, AND IT IS ALSO A FIX.  All three engines AGREE here.  Measured on
-- cold builds either side of F-3a-ii:
--     BASE  run 111 (exit 0) | native 111 (exit 0)   -- both WRONG: the shattered goal
--                                                       arg-0-matched the decoy
--     PR    run 107 (exit 0) | native 107 (exit 0)   -- both RIGHT
-- So the decoy alone does NOT cause the sibling's `run` panic -- with the decoy still
-- in place, eval selects correctly and executes.  Combined with
-- nary_sig_requires_no_decoy (no decoy, `requires` present, also fine), the panic
-- requires BOTH: a decoy in the candidate set AND a winner carrying a `requires`
-- context.  Neither discriminator alone would have told you that.
data Box c = MkBox c

interface Ix a b where
  ix : a -> b -> Int

impl Ix Int Bool where
  ix _ _ = 111

impl Ix Int (Box c) where
  ix _ _ = 107

useIx : Ix a (Box Int) => a -> Int
useIx x = ix x (MkBox 7)

main = println (useIx 1)
